    pdf_claim="Guns on Powers Hill on the morning of 3 Jul fired at Culp's Hill; emplacement not described (open-ground positions, logged as a gap); Meade moved to Powers Hill after the Leister house became untenable.",
    corrected=("Confirmed with additions and one correction of time. The Twelfth Corps artillery that fired on Culp's Hill on the morning of 3 Jul stood on two heights and by the Baltimore pike. Knap's Pennsylvania battery (Lt. Atwell; six 10-pounder Parrotts) was 'placed on a knob situated southwest of the pike 100 yards from it, and known on that day as Slocum's Hill' (Muhlenberg), 'on the hill known as Slocum's headquarters, and near the Baltimore pike' (Hunt); Winegar's Battery M, 1st New York (four 10-pounders) stood 'on a second elevation, a quarter of a mile distant, and nearly due east' (Muhlenberg), 'at a short distance east of it' (Hunt); both overlooked the ground the corps had left and stayed there through the whole engagement. Muhlenberg's F, 4th U.S. (six 12-pounders) and Kinzie's K, 5th U.S. (four 12-pounders) were put in position at 1 a.m. on 3 Jul on the southwest side of the pike opposite the centre of the corps line. Hunt adds a fifth battery, Rigby's A, Maryland (six 3-inch), 'placed on the hill' soon after daylight; Rigby himself puts his battery on a hill about a mile south of Gettysburg and 500 yards west of the pike and says he shelled the woods from daylight for about three hours (Tyler: by Slocum's direction). "
               "The Official Records never use the name Powers Hill. Sources outside the OR do: 'Powers' Hill is where General Slocum had his head-quarters' (Bachelder 1873, who adds that the Twelfth Corps batteries were sent to Powers' and Culp's hills); the 1940 National Park Service study, following the Comte de Paris, puts Knap's on Power's Hill and Winegar's on McAllister's Hill, a quarter of a mile apart; the Park Service's 1999 General Management Plan says 'Before 4 a.m., Twelfth Corps artillery at Powers Hill and along the Baltimore Pike opened fire against Confederate positions on the south slope and ridge of Culp's Hill'. The history of Battery M (1889) differs: its right section stood on Powers' Hill and its left on the McAllister farm hill. "
               "Opening clock: 3.30 a.m. (Geary), 3.45 (Candy), 4.00 (Slocum, Greene, Packer: 'twenty pieces'), 4.30 (Muhlenberg, Hunt), 'daylight' (Williams); the first fire lasted ten minutes (Geary) or fifteen (Muhlenberg, Hunt, Williams); all the batteries fired at intervals from 5.30 to about 10 a.m. (Muhlenberg, Hunt). Guns: 20 (Muhlenberg, Packer) or 26 with Rigby (Hunt). No work (earthwork, gun pit, epaulement) is described for any of these batteries in any text read: the PDF's 'not described' stands. "
               "Meade's move is documented but belongs to the afternoon cannonade, not to the morning fire: the Leister farmhouse was hit (a shell burst among the staff horses tied to the fence), Meade and staff withdrew to the fenced yard behind the house, headquarters then moved to a barn several hundred yards down the Taneytown Road, and after a shell struck Butterfield there Meade and staff 'removed to General Slocum's head-quarters on Powers Hill' (Meade's son, 1913; also Toombs 1888 and New York at Gettysburg 1900). The cannonade opened at 1 p.m. and lasted about three hours (Meade's own dispatch). 'Untenable' is the PDF's word: the sources say the house was too exposed and that no staff officer could reach headquarters from the corps commanders; the Park Service Leister page says only that Meade 'evacuated to a new headquarters'."),
